!|F005208|c00|Y01000300|@4605MESSERSCHMITT BF 109E-3|Y02000500
!|@194CBF 109E-3,II/JAGDGESCHWADER 77, LUFTWAFFE, AALBORG, NORWAY, JULY 1940
!|Y02000400
!|@0K55One of the greatest fighter aircraft of WWII.  The BF 109 was a mainsta\
y of the Lufwaffe from the Spanish
!|@075GCivil War right up until the defeat of the Third Reich.  Nearly thirty-\
five thousand BF 109s were produced,
!|@075Rmore than any other fighter of that era.  BF 109s saw service in nearly\
 every German offensive of WWII, and
!|@0762were the only German single-seat fighters used in the Battle of Britain\
.  There the BF 109s, with their small
!|@076Dstubby wings, were an even match for the Spitfire, and swifter and more\
 maneuverable than any of the other
!|@076OBritish fighters.
!|@076Z  The BF 109E-3 was developed in 1939 as a faster, more heavily-armed i\
mprovement over the earlier models,
!|@077Athe BF 109B and C, which had been deadly in the Spanish Civil War.  (Al\
though designed by Willy Messer-
!|@077Lschmitt, the BF 109 was named after his company at the time, Bayerische\
 Flugzeugwerke.)  The 109 featured
!|@077Wa more powerful and reliable Daimler Benz 601Aa engine, as well as 20mm\
 cannons in the wings in place of
!|@0787machine guns.  The BF 109E-3 saw service in the campaigns against Franc\
e, the Netherlands, Belgium, and
!|@078ILuxembourg, where it gained a fearsome reputation.|c0B|LFO39FO3K
